<h2>The allegations and the petition</h2>
<p>The parties had known each other for about two years. The FIR — No. 32/2026, PS Rajinder Nagar — alleged that the petitioner gave the complainant a ring, promised marriage, induced a physical relationship, then distanced himself and threatened to circulate her private pictures. Her statement under Section 183 BNSS recorded the refusal to marry; her Section 180 BNSS statements added allegations of nude photographs and blackmail. The petitioner called it a consensual relationship that failed, pointed to delay in the FIR, and relied on precedents where proceedings had been quashed after the parties married or moved on.</p>

<h2>The court\'s reasoning</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Prima facie offence disclosed:</strong> the FIR and chargesheet, read with the complainant\'s statements, alleged a specific marriage assurance that led to the relationship — not a bare breakdown. At the threshold stage that is enough to keep the case outside the quashing categories.</li>
  <li><strong>Bad faith versus breach:</strong> the court restated the governing distinction — a promise defeated by unforeseen circumstances is not Section 69 BNS; a promise made with no intention of performance, which directly induced the relationship, is. Which side of the line this case falls on is a trial question.</li>
  <li><strong>Precedents distinguished:</strong> in the decisions relied upon, the parties had married after the FIR or the complainant had moved on; neither was true here.</li>
  <li><strong>Conduct counted:</strong> the petitioner had been declared a proclaimed person on 15 September 2026 for deliberately avoiding warrants and had not joined the investigation — a factor the court expressly weighed in declining to entertain the petition.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>The strategic lesson</h2>
<p>Accused persons sometimes treat quashing petitions and absconding as parallel tracks: stay out of reach while the High Court considers the FIR. This judgment shows why that strategy backfires. Discretionary jurisdictions — Section 528 BNSS, anticipatory bail under Section 482 BNSS — are exercised for those who submit to the process. Once a proclaimed person declaration enters the record, every court that touches the file sees an accused who has defied process, and relief at every level becomes harder: quashing is refused, anticipatory bail is viewed with suspicion, and the separate offence of non-appearance under Section 209 BNS can be added. The sounder sequence is almost always the reverse — join the investigation, contest the proclamation if it was irregularly issued, secure bail, and then attack the FIR on its merits with clean hands.</p>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> Where a client learns of a warrant in a Section 69 BNS case, act before the proclamation stage: an application for anticipatory bail, or appearance with an application for exemption, keeps the discretionary doors open. After a proclamation, the realistic first step is surrender and regular bail — not a quashing petition.</p>
</div>
